# MASTER DEVELOPMENT DIRECTIVE
## OPES HealthCore — Universal Healthcare & Life Sciences Reusable SDK

**Repository:** https://github.com/exerateanalytical/resuseable-emr-ehr-hie-features

**Primary stack:** Java 25, Spring Boot 4, Maven, PostgreSQL, Kotlin Multiplatform for optional mobile SDKs.

**Mission:** Transform OPES HealthCore into a complete, modular, independently reusable software development foundation for healthcare, pharmaceutical and life-science developers.

---

# 1. CRITICAL PRODUCT DEFINITION

OPES HealthCore is NOT a Hospital Information System, EMR, EHR, HIE, pharmacy application, diagnostic-center application or pharmaceutical manufacturing application.

It is a **reusable SDK, framework and module library** from which independent software developers can build those applications.

The purpose is to eliminate repeated implementation of complex, common healthcare and life-science functionality.

Developers must be able to select the modules relevant to their industry, integrate them into their existing software, configure them and build their own user-facing products.

### Mandatory architectural principles

1. **Composable:** Each module must work independently with only its documented dependencies.
2. **Domain-specific:** Pharmaceutical, clinical, diagnostic and research capabilities must have appropriate models and workflows.
3. **Configurable:** Business rules, jurisdictions, workflows, terminology, databases and policies must be customizable.
4. **Extensible:** Developers must be able to override default behavior through stable extension interfaces.
5. **Headless-first:** Modules must work without any reference UI.
6. **Frontend-independent:** Expose REST/OpenAPI and Java service interfaces. Support integration from Java, Kotlin, Python, .NET, JavaScript-based applications and other systems through documented APIs.
7. **Production-oriented:** All advertised features must have implemented persistence, security, errors and tests.
8. **Standards-aware:** Support relevant international standards without falsely claiming certification.
9. **Offline-capable where relevant:** Offline support must be optional, not an unnecessary requirement for every module.
10. **Backward-compatible:** Modules must use explicit versions and published compatibility rules.

Never require a developer building a standalone pharmacy or diagnostic-center application to install the whole hospital reference application.

